{
 "summary": "Findings: The plan introduces a store/scheduler dependency cycle, persists derived queue state, and omits the queue transitions needed to keep the settings display correct. It also assumes a nonexistent timer, adds unreachable validation, and omits the promised local-time formatting.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "Title": "Keep derived delivery state out of persisted preferences",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t2 (plan.md:50)",
   "Failure": "Single Source of Truth: nextDeliveryAt depends on the scheduler's in-memory queue and current quiet hours, but the plan gives it a second, persisted home in Preferences. PreferencesStore persists its complete value (src/preferences/store.ts:18-21), while every scheduler starts with an empty queue (src/notify/scheduler.ts:25-28). A saved delivery timestamp can therefore survive a restart without the notifications it describes. This also expands the preference input schema and sender payload unnecessarily: Sender receives Preferences (src/notify/scheduler.ts:8-9).",
   "Fix": "Keep the queue and its derived delivery time under scheduler ownership. Let settings read that derived value rather than adding it to Preferences or parsePreferences. Verify that a fresh scheduler displays no delivery time."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Remove the store-to-scheduler-to-store update cycle",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t1 and phase-1/t2 (plan.md:38,50)",
   "Failure": "Bidirectional Coupling: attachScheduler makes PreferencesStore invoke the scheduler, while the new scheduler constructor and reschedule write back through that same store. The proposed #rescheduling flag exists solely to interrupt this new cycle. The current dependency direction already lets the scheduler read preferences through a callback (src/notify/scheduler.ts:28), the store already exposes subscriptions (src/preferences/store.ts:24-26), and createApp owns composition (src/app.ts:6-10). No existing store operation requires notification-specific knowledge (src/preferences/store.ts:10-26).",
   "Fix": "Keep the store independent of NotificationScheduler. Compose an explicitly awaited preference-change reaction at the application boundary, preserving the requirement that update resolves only after reevaluation. Existing listeners return void and are not awaited (src/preferences/store.ts:3,21), so simply subscribing an async callback is not sufficient. Remove the reverse store write and recursion flag."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Update the displayed delivery time on queue changes, not only preference saves",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "phase-1/t2 and phase-2/t2 (plan.md:50,59,91,97-98)",
   "Failure": "The plan computes nextDeliveryAt only in reschedule, and only store.update calls reschedule. Holding a notification changes only #held (src/notify/scheduler.ts:30-35); flushing removes held notifications without updating preferences (src/notify/scheduler.ts:40-43). Therefore a notification held after the last save never produces the promised display value, and a normal flush never clears a previously recorded value. The proposed fields() tests require both behaviors but the implementation tasks provide neither path.",
   "Fix": "Derive the settings value from current scheduler queue state and preferences, or explicitly cover every queue transition if retaining cached state. Add tests that hold a notification without saving preferences, then release it through the ordinary flush path and verify both settings states."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Do not let the recursion flag suppress a separate preference save",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t2 and implementation_contract/d1 (plan.md:50,115-117)",
   "Failure": "The plan skips reschedule whenever a store-wide #rescheduling flag is set, without distinguishing the internal timestamp write from another user save. There is no save serialization: SettingsForm.submit directly awaits store.update (src/ui/settings-form.ts:17-18), and update independently awaits persistence (src/preferences/store.ts:18-21). Flush also awaits Sender.send after capturing preferences (src/notify/scheduler.ts:41-43), so rescheduling contains an asynchronous interval. UNVERIFIED runtime claim: a concurrent save during that interval would complete without its own reevaluation, contradicting d1. The proposed code is not present to execute, and the plan supplies no exclusion mechanism or test.",
   "Fix": "Remove the internal preference write and its recursion flag. Ensure each supported save awaits its own reevaluation; include an overlapping-save test with a controlled pending sender or persistence promise."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Resolve the nonexistent minute-timer premise",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "context.constraints and phase-1 verification (plan.md:18,64)",
   "Failure": "The plan says the minute timer calling flush stays, but createApp only constructs and returns objects (src/app.ts:6-10). No timer is registered anywhere in the supplied source. The scheduler comment describes a timer (src/notify/scheduler.ts:39), but the only actual flush invocation in the project is a manual test call (tests/scheduler.test.ts:10). Thus the plan has no demonstrated ordinary release path when a still-active quiet window later ends.",
   "Fix": "Correct the premise. Identify and verify the external timer owner if one exists outside this project; otherwise include the minimal application scheduling and disposal path required for ordinary delivery. Verify that path without manually calling flush in the acceptance test."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Remove unreachable quiet-hours fallback logic",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-2/t1 (plan.md:79-86)",
   "Failure": "Speculative Code: the proposed scheduler fallback handles missing or malformed quiet hours that supported producers cannot emit. Preferences requires quietHours (src/preferences/parse.ts:9-12); parsePreferences validates both fields and defaults invalid inputs (src/preferences/parse.ts:29-39). The store parses saved input and every update (src/preferences/store.ts:10-11,18-19), and createApp supplies scheduler preferences exclusively through that store (src/app.ts:7-8). Existing tests already cover corrupt saved input (tests/parse.test.ts:4-6). The plan gives no evidence for a second validation boundary or its added fallback policy.",
   "Fix": "Delete phase-2/t1. Preserve validation at parsePreferences and retain the existing parser regression coverage."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Specify and verify local-time formatting for the settings value",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-2/t2 and implementation_contract/d2 (plan.md:91,97-98,120-122)",
   "Failure": "The plan stores an ISO timestamp (plan.md:50) and then reads that string into nextDelivery while promising 'Held notifications arrive at HH:MM' (plan.md:91). It provides no conversion from the ISO timestamp to the user's local HH:MM. SettingsForm.fields is the entire supplied screen representation and currently returns raw field values (src/ui/settings-form.ts:3-6,12-15); there is no downstream renderer that performs this conversion. Tests that merely assert a nextDelivery value do not prove the promised display.",
   "Fix": "Specify local HH:MM formatting at the settings boundary. Assert the exact displayed value for a known local delivery time, as well as its absence when the queue is empty."
  }
 ],
 "review_scope": "Read the complete plan and all seven source/test files, inspected package.json, and searched all project call sites for timers, flush, subscriptions, updates, and constructor wiring. This was a read-only plan review; no implementation or tests were run."
}

{
 "summary": "Findings: Four defects require plan corrections: unnecessary cache-migration machinery, unspecified handling of inherited tag keys, a shell-invalid acceptance command, and an incorrect claim about posting order. Reviewed the complete plan and all project source, tests, and package configuration. No files changed.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "Title": "Use the existing cache rebuild instead of adding a temporary migration subsystem",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t2 (plan.md:49-60)",
   "Failure": "Speculative Code: The plan adds a legacy schema, converter, dedicated tests, loader branch, and two-release removal obligation for a disposable cache. The existing loader already rebuilds incompatible versions and writes the replacement (src/search/index-file.ts:12-21). Notes explicitly remain the source of truth (src/notes/store.ts:20), and the CLI reads every note before loading the index (src/cli.ts:9-10). The converter therefore preserves no otherwise-lost data and avoids only the in-memory word-index construction (src/search/build.ts:21-28). The plan supplies no performance evidence that justifies this additional compatibility lifecycle.",
   "Fix": "Remove the converter task, module, legacy type, and sunset obligation. Retain the existing version-mismatch rebuild path. Extend tests/index-file.test.ts:11-18 with a matching-fingerprint version-1 cache and verify that loading rebuilds and persists version 2 with tags. If avoiding word reconstruction is a required performance constraint, establish it before retaining the migration subsystem."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Define safe storage and lookup for unrestricted tag names",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t1 and phase-2/t1 (plan.md:38,77)",
   "Failure": "The plan introduces a string-keyed tag table and direct lookup without addressing inherited object properties. The supported parser accepts tags named constructor and __proto__ unchanged (src/notes/store.ts:14-17). Reusing the existing plain-object table initialization and nullish-assignment insertion pattern (src/search/build.ts:22-25) throws for both names. Direct lookup followed by the existing Set conversion (src/search/query.ts:7) also throws for these absent keys after JSON loading (src/search/index-file.ts:11,17). Fresh Bun probes confirmed both insertion failures and both lookup failures, including lookup after JSON serialization and parsing of a null-prototype table. These tags currently parse and build successfully when they occur only in metadata.",
   "Fix": "Specify prototype-safe tag construction and own-property lookup. Null-prototype construction alone does not protect a JSON-loaded table. Add behavior tests for constructor and __proto__ tags, absent reserved-name queries, and search after a cache round trip. Apply the same tag-construction rule to the converter if it remains."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Quote hashtag queries in the executable acceptance check",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-2 (plan.md:91; also success_criteria at plan.md:95)",
   "Failure": "The prescribed command, notes #garden, does not pass a query in Bash or noninteractive Zsh: #garden begins a shell comment. Fresh probes in both shells confirmed zero arguments; quoting produced one argument. The CLI joins its arguments into the query (src/cli.ts:8), and search returns no results for an empty query (src/search/query.ts:5-6). The proposed acceptance command therefore cannot verify the new behavior in these execution environments.",
   "Fix": "Use notes '#garden' in the acceptance check and command examples. Include a quoted mixed query, such as notes '#garden tomatoes', when checking CLI integration."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Correct the claim that index postings are sorted",
   "Severity": "P3",
   "Location": "phase-1/t1 (context claim at plan.md:14)",
   "Failure": "The context claims that index tables contain sorted note paths. buildIndex instead appends paths in its caller's input order without sorting (src/search/build.ts:21-28). listNotes sorts filenames for the CLI path (src/notes/store.ts:22-23), but the builder itself has no sorted-input contract. A fresh probe with z.md followed by a.md produced words.tomatoes equal to [z.md, a.md]. Only search explicitly sorts its returned paths (src/search/query.ts:8-9).",
   "Fix": "Correct the context to describe input-order postings and sorted search results. Keep tag-result sorting at the search boundary; do not introduce posting sorts solely to satisfy this inaccurate description."
  }
 ],
 "verification": "Read every existing project file. Ran focused Bun probes for parser acceptance, posting order, ignored plain-query tokens, prototype-key insertion, and JSON-round-trip lookup. Ran focused Bash and Zsh argument probes for the acceptance command. No project-wide gate ran because this was a read-only plan review."
}

{
 "summary": "Findings: Four defects require plan changes. The existing fan-out test would exceed Bun's timeout; the plan duplicates the retry engine; its classifier retries failures outside the stated classes; and its tests omit network failures. No files were changed.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "Title": "The existing fan-out failure test will exceed its timeout",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t2; plan.md:50-61",
   "Failure": "The plan replaces the single attempt without accounting for the existing test's permanently failing endpoint. tests/fanout.test.ts:4-7 always returns 500 for that endpoint and awaits fanOut. src/webhooks/fanout.ts:10-17 awaits every endpoint through Promise.all. The proposed loop at plan.md:35 adds 500 + 1000 + 2000 + 4000 = 7500 ms of sleeps before returning failure. The installed Bun's `bun test --help` confirms a 5000 ms default per-test timeout. The current test passes freshly, but the planned behavior cannot complete this fixture within that timeout.",
   "Fix": "Specify how tests avoid real exhaustion delays. The existing outcome-isolation test can use a permanent 400 failure; retry-exhaustion tests can use the proposed injected sleep. Test concurrency by observing the healthy delivery while the retrying endpoint remains pending, not by expecting an early result from Promise.all."
  },
  {
   "Title": "The plan creates a second retry engine instead of extending the existing owner",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t1; plan.md:35",
   "Failure": "Single Source of Truth: The new loop independently owns attempt counting, retry classification, exponential delays, sleeping, and final-error propagation. src/lib/retry.ts:12-27 already owns those mechanisms, and src/email/sender.ts:15-23 already consumes that owner. There is a real policy difference: src/lib/retry.ts:22-23 adds up to 20 percent jitter, whereas plan.md:35 and :41 require exact delays. The plan neither resolves that difference within the existing mechanism nor explains why webhook delivery needs a separate engine.",
   "Fix": "Keep one retry engine. Extend the existing helper with the smallest explicit timing-policy choice needed for exact webhook delays, preserving the current email jitter default. Implement webhook-specific classification and configuration around that helper, and verify both timing policies."
  },
  {
   "Title": "The retry classifier treats non-transient failures as retryable",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t1; plan.md:35; implementation_contract/d1, plan.md:75",
   "Failure": "The directive identifies network failures, 429, and 5xx as retryable, but the planned predicate returns true for everything except most 4xx errors. src/webhooks/dispatcher.ts:26-32 can throw before fetch during serialization or signing and creates WebhookStatusError for every non-success response, not only 4xx and 5xx. RelayEvent.payload is unrestricted unknown at src/webhooks/dispatcher.ts:6-9. A fresh call to the actual dispatcher with a cyclic payload produced TypeError with zero fetch calls; injected 302 and 304 Responses both produced WebhookStatusError. The proposed predicate retries all those cases and adds the full backoff to failures that are not the specified transient delivery classes.",
   "Fix": "Specify the classification boundary explicitly. Retry HTTP status errors only for 429 and 500-599. Distinguish fetch rejection from serialization/signing failure so local preparation errors are not classified as network failures. Add tests for those reachable exclusions."
  },
  {
   "Title": "The required network-failure class has no planned test",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t1; plan.md:40-45; implementation_contract/d1, plan.md:75-76",
   "Failure": "The completion check promises coverage of each retryable class, but all listed retry scenarios use HTTP responses. No scenario makes fetch reject. Existing tests do not close the gap: tests/fanout.test.ts:5 only returns Responses, while tests/retry.test.ts:4-21 exercises generic operations without the dispatcher or webhook classifier. HTTP-status tests cannot verify that a fetch rejection reaches and is accepted by the webhook retry policy.",
   "Fix": "Add a webhook test in which the injected fetcher rejects and then succeeds. Assert the calls and injected delay. Include exhausted network failures if final-error propagation differs from the status-error path."
  }
 ],
 "verification": {
  "reviewed": "Read the complete plan, package.json, all four source files, and both existing test files.",
  "checks": [
   "bun test tests/fanout.test.ts: 1 passed, 0 failed.",
   "bun test --help: confirmed the installed runtime's 5000 ms default per-test timeout.",
   "Called the actual deliverWebhook with injected 302, 304, 429, and 503 Responses: each threw WebhookStatusError with the corresponding status.",
   "Called the actual deliverWebhook with a cyclic payload: TypeError occurred before any fetch call."
  ],
  "limits": "The implementation does not exist yet. The planned timeout failure follows from its explicit sleep schedule, the current test fixture, and the runtime's documented timeout; the future implementation was not executed."
 }
}

{
 "summary": "Findings: Four defects need correction: phase 1 breaks existing consumers, the legacy-reader removal date conflicts with upgrade compatibility, final manual acceptance has no runnable application entrypoint, and the context overstates loader warning behavior.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "Title": "Keep the settings schema change and its consumers in one coherent milestone",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t1; phase-2/t2 (plan.md:41, 66, 98, 113)",
   "Failure": "The first phase removes ShopSettings.currencySymbol, but updates its consumers only in phase 2. Cart formatting reads that property at src/cart/summary.ts:12-13; invoice rendering reads it at src/invoices/render.ts:11; receipt formatting reads it at src/emails/receipt.ts:6. The existing receipt test also constructs the removed shape at tests/receipt.test.ts:5. A fresh Bun probe passing the planned CAD settings shape to these unchanged consumers produced 'undefined12.50' in the cart, '12.50 undefined' on the invoice, and TypeError: currency must be a string in the receipt. The phase-1 settings-only gate does not exercise these failures.",
   "Fix": "Combine the schema replacement, shared formatter, all three consumer migrations, and affected tests into one milestone. Run bun test at that boundary. Do not add temporary duplicated currency state to preserve the proposed split."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Remove the unsupported expiration date for legacy settings compatibility",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t2 (plan.md:53; compatibility commitments at plan.md:22, 120, 128)",
   "Failure": "The required comment instructs removal of the legacy reader after 2027-06-30, although nothing establishes that old settings expire or migrate by that date. README.md:3 states that each release reads settings written by earlier releases. The only production save caller is submitSettings at src/settings/page.ts:14-16; loadSettings only reads and returns settings at src/settings/load.ts:26-37. The plan itself postpones writing the new shape until the next settings-page save. A shop that does not open settings can therefore retain a legacy file past the date. Removing the reader would lose its EUR or GBP selection on a later upgrade. Neither version 3.0 nor the cutoff has supporting project metadata; package.json:1-8 contains no version.",
   "Fix": "Keep the legacy mapping without a calendar removal instruction. Explain that it reads settings written by earlier releases. Remove it only when an independently established migration and support policy makes legacy files unreachable."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Replace the nonexistent app-start acceptance step with a runnable integration check",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-2 qualitative_verification (plan.md:114)",
   "Failure": "The plan requires starting the app and checking its cart without visiting settings, but this project exposes only library functions and tests. package.json:5-7 defines only the test command. The relevant entrypoints are exported functions at src/settings/load.ts:26, src/cart/summary.ts:9, src/invoices/render.ts:10, and src/emails/receipt.ts:5; there is no application bootstrap or start command in the supplied tree. The separate loader and formatter scenarios do not specify the full old-file-to-rendered-output acceptance path.",
   "Fix": "Replace this step with a Bun integration test that writes an earlier-release settings fixture, calls loadSettings, and passes its result to the actual renderers without calling submitSettings. Assert the preserved currency and locale-aware output. Include this test in the milestone verification rather than adding an application launcher."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Correct the claimed loader warning contract",
   "Severity": "P3",
   "Location": "phase-1/t2 and context.key_files/context.patterns (plan.md:8, 16, 53)",
   "Failure": "The context claims every missing or invalid field takes its default with a warning. Actual behavior differs: src/settings/load.ts:31 silently defaults an invalid or missing shopName, and src/settings/load.ts:32-34 silently defaults a missing locale. Currency-symbol validation does warn for a missing or invalid symbol at src/settings/load.ts:35-37. The blanket claim misstates the baseline that the migration is supposed to preserve.",
   "Fix": "Document the field-specific behavior accurately. Keep the currency migration warning policy explicit without implying that existing shopName and locale validation already follows the same policy."
  }
 ],
 "verified_nonfindings": [
  "The current choices are exactly $, €, and £ at src/settings/choices.ts:2-3. Both existing symbol-to-code maps agree on USD, EUR, and GBP at src/invoices/render.ts:8 and src/emails/receipt.ts:3.",
  "All current amount-rendering sites are covered by the proposed formatter migration: cart lines and total at src/cart/summary.ts:12-13, invoice lines and total at src/invoices/render.ts:15-16, and receipt amount at src/emails/receipt.ts:6.",
  "A fresh Bun Intl.NumberFormat probe confirmed every explicit formatting expectation at plan.md:91-93, including CAD locale disambiguation and the U+00A0 space in de-DE.",
  "The existing settings test owns invalid-symbol fallback and warning behavior at tests/settings.test.ts:10-17; the existing receipt test owns locale-aware receipt formatting at tests/receipt.test.ts:4-5. The plan identifies both existing test files correctly."
 ]
}

{
 "summary": "Findings: five defects require plan corrections. The main structural defect is a store/scheduler feedback loop that persists queue-derived state. The plan also omits delivery-time updates during normal queue changes, adds unreachable input hardening, assumes a nonexistent timer, and leaves display formatting unspecified.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "Title": "Remove the store/scheduler feedback loop and persisted delivery metadata",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t1 and phase-1/t2; plan.md:38,50",
   "Failure": "One-Direction Data Flow: the plan makes PreferencesStore invoke the scheduler, then makes the scheduler write back through PreferencesStore.update. The #rescheduling flag exists solely to stop this new cycle. Today the store owns parsed preferences and persistence (src/preferences/store.ts:5-21), while the scheduler only reads preferences and owns the held queue (src/notify/scheduler.ts:24-28). Each planned reschedule adds another persistence operation and listener notification through update (src/preferences/store.ts:18-21). Persisting nextDeliveryAt also gives queue-derived data an independent lifetime: a new scheduler starts with an empty queue (src/notify/scheduler.ts:26), while the proposed parser would restore the saved timestamp. The settings screen could therefore advertise delivery with nothing held.",
   "Fix": "Keep preference persistence independent of the scheduler. Wire an awaited preference-change notification at the composition root so saving still waits for re-evaluation. Keep delivery metadata with the scheduler and expose a derived query to the settings form. Remove nextDeliveryAt from Preferences, the nested store.update, and #rescheduling."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Update the displayed delivery time on queue changes, not only preference saves",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "phase-1/t2 and phase-2/t2; plan.md:50,58-60,91-98",
   "Failure": "The only planned producer of nextDeliveryAt is reschedule, and the only planned caller is PreferencesStore.update. Normal notification holding happens in notify without updating preferences (src/notify/scheduler.ts:30-35). Normal release happens in flush without updating preferences (src/notify/scheduler.ts:40-44). Consequently, holding a notification without saving settings leaves the display unset, and a later ordinary flush leaves the stored display stale. This contradicts the planned fields() scenarios at plan.md:97-98.",
   "Fix": "Make the delivery-time query reflect the scheduler's current queue and current preferences, including after notify and flush. Add scenarios that hold and release notifications through those existing methods without saving settings, then assert the settings fields."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Remove unreachable quiet-hours fallback logic",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-2/t1; plan.md:69,79-86",
   "Failure": "Speculative Code: the plan adds missing-field and malformed-time handling inside isQuiet, although its Preferences input requires quietHours (src/preferences/parse.ts:9-12). Both loading and updating preferences already pass through parsePreferences (src/preferences/store.ts:10-11,18-19). That parser validates each time and supplies defaults (src/preferences/parse.ts:19,29-39). The production scheduler receives only store.get() values (src/app.ts:7-8). No supported producer shown in this project supplies either proposed invalid state. The new tests would bypass the existing input contract instead of protecting the requested behavior.",
   "Fix": "Delete phase-2/t1. Preserve parsing and validation at parsePreferences, as the plan's unchanged-parsing constraint requires. Existing invalid-input coverage belongs in tests/parse.test.ts:4-6."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Resolve the unsupported claim that an existing minute timer will remain",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "context.constraints and phase-1 verification; plan.md:18,64",
   "Failure": "The plan says that the minute timer calling flush stays. The supplied application only constructs and returns the store, scheduler, and form (src/app.ts:6-10). No timer is registered anywhere in the supplied project. The scheduler comment says a timer calls flush (src/notify/scheduler.ts:39), but the only actual flush call outside its implementation is a direct test call (tests/scheduler.test.ts:10). A comment does not establish the timer boundary, so the plan cannot claim that ordinary automatic delivery remains wired or verified.",
   "Fix": "Identify the actual external timer owner and its invocation contract, with a verification path. If this project must own automatic flushing, explicitly account for the missing wiring rather than describing it as existing behavior."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Specify and verify the promised local-time display formatting",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-2/t2 and implementation_contract/d2; plan.md:50,91-98,120-122",
   "Failure": "The plan defines nextDeliveryAt as an ISO timestamp, then directs FormFields.nextDelivery to read that value while promising 'Held notifications arrive at HH:MM'. The supplied settings surface only returns fields; there is no downstream renderer or formatter (src/ui/settings-form.ts:3-15). The plan identifies no step that converts the ISO value to local HH:MM or produces the promised display text. Its completion check merely asserts nextDelivery, which could pass for an unformatted ISO timestamp.",
   "Fix": "Specify the formatting contract at the actual settings fields boundary. Assert the expected local HH:MM value or complete display text, rather than only the presence of nextDelivery."
  }
 ],
 "verified_scope": "Read the complete plan, every source file, both existing test files, and package.json. Verified all named existing files and surfaces, constructor callers, preference-input boundaries, queue mutation paths, settings submission, persistence/listener behavior, and the absence of timer registration. The new store and settings test files are planned additions, not existing coverage. Review was read-only; no tests were run."
}

{
 "summary": "Findings: four P2 defects. The plan duplicates existing aggregation, adds a forwarding-only exporter, misstates the missing-range response contract, and leaves successful route data flow untested.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "Title": "Reuse the existing daily aggregation instead of creating groupByDay",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t2 (plan.md:46)",
   "Failure": "Single Source of Truth: the proposed local groupByDay duplicates salesByDay, which already groups by the store-local date, counts orders, sums quantity and revenue cents, and sorts days (src/reports/sales.ts:19-27). The HTML renderer consumes that canonical aggregation (src/reports/sales.ts:30-32). A separate implementation makes HTML and CSV daily totals independently maintained, despite plan.md:6 explicitly identifying salesByDay.",
   "Fix": "Import salesByDay and map its SalesDay results into CSV cells. Keep only CSV-specific formatting in salesCsv."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Remove the forwarding-only CsvExporter class",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t1, phase-1/t2, phase-2/t1 (plan.md:36-41,46,71)",
   "Failure": "Shallow Wrapper: CsvExporter.export only forwards identical arguments to toCsv. The existing function already owns CSV encoding (src/format/csv.ts:1-7), and stockCsv imports it directly (src/reports/stock.ts:1,8-9). The class adds an object parameter, route-level construction, a file, and an equivalence test without adding behavior or hiding complexity. It also makes the HTTP route coordinate an encoding dependency, unlike the existing report calls (src/http/report-routes.ts:18-23).",
   "Fix": "Have salesCsv(sales) call toCsv directly, following stockCsv. Remove the exporter file, constructor wiring, parameter, and forwarding-equivalence test."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Correct the missing-range test to use the handler's rejection contract",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-2/t1 (plan.md:78)",
   "Failure": "The claim that a missing range 'returns 400, as for the HTML report' does not describe handleReport's callable contract. range throws a Response (src/http/report-routes.ts:9-13), and handleReport does not catch it (src/http/report-routes.ts:16-25). A fresh Bun probe confirmed that a valid HTML request resolves with status 200, but a request without the range rejects with a Response whose status is 400. The project contains no server adapter that establishes conversion of this rejection into an HTTP response. A test expecting an ordinarily returned 400 Response would fail.",
   "Fix": "Specify that the handler-level test catches the rejected Response and asserts status 400, preserving HTML parity. If actual HTTP 400 behavior is required, identify the serving entrypoint and its error-conversion boundary before claiming that behavior."
  },
  {
